The Fingberg–Heller–Karsch plaquette operator is (1/N)Tr U_p, which is (1/2)Tr U_p for SU(2). Their Table 3 is a compilation from earlier work, so I did not trace the underlying primary papers.

**Size matching** (`match.py`, output in `match_out.txt`): l√σ = N·a√σ, using the Fingberg–Heller–Karsch values for β = 2.2 to 2.7. Four pairs with N_f = 2N_c agree within 5%, out of 72 candidate pairs:
- (2.3, 6)–(2.5, 12): +3.25%
- (2.3, 8)–(2.5, 16): +3.25%
- (2.4, 6)–(2.6, 12): +2.26%
- (2.4, 8)–(2.6, 16): +2.26%

Only 2 of the 6 coarse cases (β_c = 2.3, 2.4, 2.5 with N_c = 6, 8) have any partner within 5% among β = 2.3 to 2.5. That failed my "every case has a partner" hypothesis, which was my own expectation, not a defect of the data. I had predicted the 2.3→2.5 match but not the 2.4→2.6 one. N_c = 12 has no partner because N_f = 24 was not requested.

**Sub-agent:** I asked it to re-locate a√σ at β = 2.5. Its control, 1 − I₂(0.5)/I₁(0.5) = 0.876282, matched, and I reread its `control.txt`. It found 0.1905(8) in Fingberg–Heller–Karsch Table 3, the same as mine. It did not identify which primary paper that table entry comes from.

